> 0.8 sqm.\nTotal surface area ~ 6 + 3 + 0.8 + 0.5 = ~10 square meters.\nIf a patch is roughly 15cm x 15cm = 0.0225 square meters. Wait, giraffe patches are not perfectly square, but let's say area of a patch is 0.02 sqm.\n10 / 0.02 = 500 patches. Wait, the patches cover about 50% of the area, or they are separated by white lines.\nLet's reconsider the number of spots per giraffe.\nActually, studies have used spot patterns to identify giraffes. How many spots are there?\nA 2018 study or similar might mention spot numbers. Let's estimate visually. A giraffe neck might have 50 spots. The torso maybe 100. Each leg maybe 20 (4 * 20 = 80). Total around 250-300 spots.\nLet's refine the area. Torso length 3.5m, circumference 2m -> Area = 7 sqm. Patches cover about 30% of area? If area of a patch is 0.02 sqm, 7 * 0.3 / 0.02 = 100 patches on torso.\nNeck: length 2.5m, circ 1m -> area 2.5 sqm. 2.5 * 0.3 / 0.02 = 40 patches.\nLegs: 4 legs, length 2m, circ 0.4m -> area 3.2 sqm. 3.2 * 0.4 / 0.02 = 64 patches.\nTotal patches ~200.\nLet's go with an average of 300 spots per giraffe.\n",
